    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/t1ula/School-Administrator,33292": {
      "title": "School Administrator",
      "description": "School Administrator -\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search School Administrator Te Kura Toi | Aotahi School of Māori and Indigenous Studies Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand 5 weeks annual leave +5 days university holidays Professional development opportunities Permanent position, 37.5 hours per week Generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) Salary $67,843 - $75,000 p.a, based on skills and experience Kia hiwa rā, kia hiwa rā! He hiahia, he pūkenga nōu ki te mahi a te School Administrator? Nāia te pōwhiri nā Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha kia tono mai i te tūranga nei. Āu Mahi | What You Will Do As School Administrator, you will play a key role in keeping Aotahi School of Māori and Indigenous Studies running smoothly, providing high-quality administrative and organisational support to the Head of School, kaimahi and tauira. This is a varied and people-focused role where you will coordinate academic and school administration, support meetings, events and wānanga, manage purchasing and travel, maintain key systems and records, and be a welcoming first point of contact for tauira, kaimahi, manuhiri and the wider community. You will bring initiative and strong organisation to keep things moving, anticipate deadlines and ensure a seamless and responsive service. Mōu | Who You Are You are an efficient, organised and proactive administrator who enjoys working with people and making things happen. You will be confident managing competing priorities, solving problems, learning new systems and building warm, respectful relationships with a diverse range of people. You will also be able to demonstrate experience in: 4+ years’ experience in office administration and/or professional support within a medium to large organisation, preferably within the tertiary sector. Building effective relationships and working confidently with people from diverse backgrounds. Strong digital capability, with confidence learning and navigating multiple systems and databases. Coordinating meetings, events, wānanga or other community-facing activities. Managing competing demands while maintaining accuracy, confidentiality and attention to detail. Working independently while contributing positively to a collaborative team environment. Cultural awareness and the ability to work respectfully and appropriately with Māori and Indigenous communities, with a working knowledge of Te Reo Māori me ōna tikanga highly desirable. An alignment with our organisation's values and culture, promoting a positive and inclusive work environment. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With In this role, you will work closely with the Head of School and the Aotahi team, supporting kaimahi and tauira while building effective relationships across the University and with manuhiri, community organisations and other external stakeholders. Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC At UC, being part of our team means more than just a job. We are committed to supporting our kaimahi | staff’s career and wellbeing with benefits designed to help our kaimahi grow and feel connected, supported and valued. 5 weeks annual leave +5 days university holidays generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) flexible working arrangements professional development opportunities supportive working environment a wide range of retail discounts across shopping, entertainment and travel on-campus staff discounts living in revitalised Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa New Zealand a unique working environment in a beautiful campus with access to UC facilities such as UC RecCentre and Staff Club at discounted rates plus onsite cafés and eateries, and more. For more information, please visit us: https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/about-uc/work-at-uc/benefits-working-uc For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/ The closing date for this position is: 29 September 2026 (midnight, NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed as they are received, and interviews may take place before the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted via our careers website and should include a cover letter and resume. Please note, we do not accept applications by email, however we are happy to answer your queries in relation to the application process, please forward these to WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz You must have Aotearoa New Zealand or Australian citizenship/permanent residency to be considered for this role. Please note, this is an onsite position. Job Details Reference # 33292 Posted on 15 Sep 2026 Closes on 29 Sep 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Administration Job level(s) Experienced Work type(s) Continuing (Permanent) full-time More details (document) PD_Aotahi School Administrator.pdf Position description 1 Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 14 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance. Loading . . ."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/IFJoa/Legal-Counsel,33845": {
      "title": "Legal Counsel",
      "description": "Legal Counsel -\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Legal Counsel Te Tari o te Tumu Whakarae | Vice-Chancellor’s Office Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand Full-time 37.5 hours per week (1.0 FTE) Continuing (permanent) position Generous leave provisions of five weeks annual leave and another five days’ special leave as University Holidays (six weeks total) Eligibility to join the University’s superannuation scheme (UniSaver) in addition to KiwiSaver (up to 6.75% employer contribution) Professional development opportunities, including study opportunities in accordance with the University’s Staff Tertiary Study Assistance Policy and Procedures Kia hiwa rā, kia hiwa rā! He hiahia, he pūkenga nōu ki te mahi a te Kaiwawao ? Nāia te pōwhiri nā Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha kia tono mai i te tūranga nei. Āu Mahi | What You Will Do An immediate opening for a full-time Legal Counsel in the Office of the Vice-Chancellor has arisen, to assist with providing commercial legal advice. You will be a key team member of UC Legal, in the commercial team,  reporting to the Senior Legal Counsel (Commercial). Mōu | Who You Are You will have a LLB Bachelor’s Degree, a current practising certificate issued by NZLS, and 5 – 7 years PQE in a broad range of commercial legal issues. In addition, you will be: a perceptive and proactive person who can provide timely advice across a full range of commercial and contractual matters excellent at relationship building with the ability to find pragmatic solutions to a variety of technical problems able to manage competing priorities and a significant and diverse work load able to work independently with limited direct supervision outstanding at writing and have excellent analytical and communication skills as well as strong judgment Applicants who have a proven record of providing the highest quality client service, and want to work in a high-performing legal team that advises a complex, large state sector organisation across a variety of portfolios should apply today. Commercial roles of this breadth do not come up in Christchurch often! Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With The UC Legal Services team is part of Te Tari o te Pouroki | Office of the General Counsel and Registrar. UC Legal Services provides independent legal advice to UC, guiding and assisting good decision-making by the University Council, the Vice-Chancellor, the Senior Leadership Team, Senior Managers and University staff on legal, strategic, commercial and risk management issues in order to assist the University in achieving its strategic goals and objectives. For more information about us, please visit our website here . Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC Ngā Uara | Our Values of manaakitanga, whanaungatanga and tiakitanga guide our decisions and behaviour and provide a roadmap for how we do things at UC, affirming our commitment to pastoral care and support for our ākonga and staff. They challenge and inspire us to be the best we can, and make UC a great place to work and study. For more info on Ngā Uara | Our Values visit us here . We are committed to accessible higher education, service to the community and the encouragement of talent without barriers of distance, wealth, class, gender or ethnicity. The University explicitly aims to produce graduates and support staff who are engaged with their communities, empowered to act for good and determined to make a difference in the world. We offer a range of fantastic benefits including flexible work practices, study opportunities and generous superannuation and leave provisions. What we offer The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Generous annual leave provisions (five weeks annual leave and another five days’ special leave as University Holidays (six weeks total)) Eligibility to join the University’s superannuation scheme (UniSaver) in addition to KiwiSaver (up to 6.75% employer contribution in addition to the salary offered) Access to an on-site Recreation Centre at discounted rates, with the new Takarua Wellness Hub expected to be completed in 2027 Professional development and study opportunities in accordance with the University’s Staff Tertiary Study Assistance Policy and Procedures Living in revitalised Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa New Zealand and a beautiful University campus with multiple on-site cafes and eateries A unique working environment with access to UC facilities such as the University Staff Club at discounted rates For more information on our Benefits, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/joinus/benefits/ For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/ The closing date for this position is: 30 September 2026 (midnight, NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed as they are received, and interviews will take place the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted via our careers website and should include a cover letter and resume. Please note, we do not accept applications by email, however we are happy to answer your queries in relation to the application process, please forward these to WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz You must have Aotearoa New Zealand or Australian citizenship/permanent residency to be considered for this role. Please note, this is an onsite position. Job Details Reference # 33845 Posted on 16 Sep 2026 Closes on 30 Sep 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Legal Job level(s) Experienced Work type(s) Continuing (Permanent) full-time More details (document) Legal Counsel_PD.pdf Position description 1 Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 12 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance. Loading . . ."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/7tKoa/Kai-rahi,33846": {
      "title": "Kaiārahi",
      "description": "Kaiārahi -\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Kaiārahi Office of Treaty Partnership | UC Māori Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand Full-time 37.5 hours per week (1.0 FTE) Permanent position Generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) Kia hiwa rā, kia hiwa rā! He hiahia, he pūkenga nōu ki te mahi a te Kaiārahi Māori ? Nāia te pōwhiri nā Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha kia tono mai i te tūranga nei. Āu Mahi | What You Will Do Are you looking for a role with lots of variety, do you enjoying working with people, are you interested in an academic career? This is the role for you! As Kaiārahi Māori for the faculty/ service Unit you will play an integral role in leading and supporting that faculty/service unit to drive strategic outputs for Māori and to embed Māori knowledge and learning content in contexts across UC. This position is also responsible for leading engagement initiatives that enhance kaimahi proficiency in bicultural competence and confidence. The role will foster and enhance the faculty's/ service unit's relationship with Ngāi Tahu and Māori communities. You will support the embedding of Tangata Tū, Tangata Ora | UC Strategy into the faculty/service unit plans and aims for development and to promote Māori achievement throughout the faculties, service units, UC and wider community. Mōu | Who You Are To be successful in this role you will hold a bachelor's degree (NZQA Level 7) or higher, with a minimum of 6 years' experience in a related role and evidence of: Demonstrated experience in professional leadership, administration and working with professionally qualified staff. Excellent listening, oral and written communication skills. Active commitment to upholding the values, tikanga, kawa and traditions as guided by mana whenua, Ngāi Tūāhuriri, and valuing te reo Māori. A high level of proficiency in te Reo Māori is an advantage. Demonstrated mentoring and coaching capability. Experience implementing and leading change. Experience in strategic planning. Proven experience working collaboratively and evidence of contribution to team success. Previous experience working in a tertiary institution (desirable). An alignment with our organisation's values and culture, promoting a positive and inclusive work environment. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With You will work with colleagues in UC Māori, and colleagues in the wider university in your areas of responsibility. Typically, you will work closely with academic/service unit staff and serve on committees. You may also work with service units and with those staff working towards student success. Ko tā UC Māori  mahi ko te ārahi i Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha whānui, otirā me matua tautoko i te taiao ako hai whakatairanga, hai whakamana hoki i te hapori kākānorua o Aotearoa nei. The role of UC Māori  is to provide cultural leadership, support and guidance to the University in general, and support a learning environment which recognises and promotes Aotearoa New Zealand's unique bicultural society. Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC At UC, being part of our team means more than just a job. We are committed to supporting our kaimahi | staff’s career and wellbeing with benefits designed to help our kaimahi grow and feel connected, supported and valued. 5 weeks annual leave +5 days university holidays generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) flexible working arrangements professional development opportunities supportive working environment a wide range of retail discounts across shopping, entertainment and travel on-campus staff discounts living in revitalised Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa New Zealand a unique working environment in a beautiful campus with access to UC facilities such as UC RecCentre and Staff Club at discounted rates plus onsite cafés and eateries, and more. For more information, please visit us: https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/about-uc/work-at-uc/benefits-working-uc For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/ The closing date for this position is: 21 September 2026 (midnight, NZ time) Please note applications will be reviewed after the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted via our careers website and should include a cover letter and resume. Please note, we do not accept applications by email, however we are happy to answer your queries in relation to the application process, please forward these to WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz You must have Aotearoa New Zealand or Australian citizenship/permanent residency to be considered for this role. Please note, this is an onsite position. #LI-DNI Job Details Reference # 33846 Posted on 16 Sep 2026 Closes on 21 Sep 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Education & Training, Te Ao Māori Job level(s) Experienced Work type(s) Continuing (Permanent) full-time More details (document) Kaiārahi Māori PD.pdf Position description 1 Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 12 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance. Loading . . ."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/jBoma/Lecturer-Teaching-Administration-in-Animation,33455": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Teaching & Administration) in Animation",
      "description": "Lecturer (Teaching & Administration) in Animation -\r\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Lecturer (Teaching & Administration) in Animation Te Kura Hanga Otinga | School of Product Design Te Kaupeka Pūhanga | Faculty of Engineering Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand Full-time | 37.5 hours per week (1.0 FTE) Continuing | Permanent Ready to help shape the next generation of digital storytellers? Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury (UC) is looking for an experienced and creative Game Arts and Animation specialist to join our academic community and help shape our exciting new Bachelor of Digital Screen (Honours) programme. Āu Mahi | What You Will Do You will play a key role in developing and delivering the Game Arts and Animation components of our new Bachelor of Digital Screen (Honours). You will: Develop and deliver engaging, industry-relevant courses in Game Arts and Animation Create an inspiring and supportive learning environment where students can develop their creative, technical and critical capabilities Bring contemporary industry practice, technologies and workflows into the classroom Develop innovative assessments and provide meaningful feedback that supports student growth Contribute to the development and evolution of the Digital Screen programme alongside colleagues across Arts and Engineering Support and supervise student creative projects and, where appropriate, postgraduate research Stay connected to emerging developments across games, animation, VFX, virtual production and digital screen practice Contribute to research and/or creative practice at Lecturer and Senior Lecturer level Pursue opportunities for external funding to support research, creative practice and student development Contribute to the wider life of the School, Faculty and University through academic and service activities. Mōu | Who You Are You are an experienced Game Arts, Animation, VFX, Digital Screen or related professional who is excited by the opportunity to share your knowledge with the next generation. You will bring strong industry experience and a genuine passion for teaching and creative practice. We are particularly interested in people with experience across areas such as: Environmental art within 3D and/or virtual production pipelines for games or film Lighting and rendering Visual effects, compositing and/or motion graphics Animation, games, VFX and motion graphics workflows 3D software such as Blender or Maya Adobe Creative Suite, particularly Photoshop, Premiere and After Effects VFX, virtual production, filmmaking and post-production technologies Physics and dynamics simulations within 3D workflows Unity and/or Unreal, particularly from an art and creative workflow perspective. You will have at least 10 years experience in the practice and/or teaching of Game Arts, Animation and Virtual Production or a related field, and/or a Bachelor's degree in a relevant discipline. A relevant postgraduate qualification would be an advantage. You will also be able to demonstrate: Experience or capability in effective tertiary teaching and learning A strong understanding of current developments in Game Arts and Animation A commitment to developing excellent undergraduate courses The ability to work effectively in multidisciplinary teams A reputation or established professional standing within your discipline A commitment to reflective practice and ongoing professional development. The University's Lecturer (Teaching and Administration) profile emphasises effective teaching, contemporary discipline expertise, reflective practice and creating learning environments where a wide range of learners can succeed. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With You will join Te Kura Hanga Otinga | School of Product Design within Te Kaupeka Pūhanga | Faculty of Engineering, working alongside existing Applied Immersive Game Design and Game Development academics. What makes this opportunity particularly exciting is the interdisciplinary nature of the new Bachelor of Digital Screen (Honours). The degree brings together six majors: Animation | Cinematic Arts | Game Arts | Game Development | Screen Sound | Screen Writing It also includes a minor in Indigenous Narrative. You will work alongside colleagues from both Arts and Engineering, creating opportunities to explore the intersections between games, film, animation, technology and digital storytelling. This is more than an opportunity to teach an established curriculum. You will help build and shape a new programme, develop courses, establish creative collaborations and influence what the future of digital screen education looks like at UC. Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC Tangata Tū, Tangata Ora - – Engaged, Empowered, Making a Difference. We are committed to accessible higher education, service to the community and the encouragement of talent without barriers of distance, wealth, class, gender or ethnicity. The University explicitly aims to produce graduates and support staff who are engaged with their communities, empowered to act for good and determined to make a difference in the world. Mea e tukua nei | What we offer The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment, this includes: relocation support five weeks annual leave and five days university holidays UC Parental Leave (up to 9 weeks paid) + 26 weeks Government paid parental leave and onsite childcare facility widely accessible sabbatical provision generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) University and Faculty scholarship support for post-graduate students a unique bequest funded endowment ( Erskine Fund ) that supports an extensive visiting fellowship programme flexible working arrangements supportive working environment professional development and study opportunities living in revitalised Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa New Zealand a unique working environment in a beautiful campus with access to UC facilities such as the recreation centre and Staff Club at discounted rates plus onsite cafés and eateries, and more. The closing date for this position is: 2 October 2026 (midnight NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed after the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted via our careers website and should include a cover letter and resume. Please note, we do not accept applications by email, however we are happy to answer your queries in relation to the application process, please forward these to WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz For overseas applicants, please visit Immigration New Zealand website, to review the latest health and visa requirements for visiting and working in New Zealand. Please note, this is an onsite position. #LI-DNI Job Details Reference # 33455 Posted on 18 Sep 2026 Closes on 02 Oct 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Lecturer Job level(s) Academic Work type(s) Continuing (Permanent) full-time More details (document) PD_Lecturer (TA)_Animation.pdf Position description 1 PD_Lecturer (TA)_Animation.pdf Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 9 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/iJema/Senior-Tutor-Game-Arts,33408": {
      "title": "Senior Tutor",
      "description": "Senior Tutor - Game Arts -\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Senior Tutor - Game Arts Te Kaupeka Pūhanga | Faculty of Engineering Kura Hanga Otinga | School of Product Design Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand Full-time (37.5 hours per week) Continuing position Kia hiwa rā, kia hiwa rā! He hiahia, he pūkenga nōu ki te mahi a te Kaiwhakaako Matua? Nāia te pōwhiri nā Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha kia tono mai i te tūranga nei. Āu Mahi | What You Will Do The School of Product Design | Kura Hanga Otinga is seeking a motivated and collaborative Senior Tutor to contribute to high-quality teaching and learning within our undergraduate and postgraduate programmes. As a Senior Tutor, you will bring disciplinary expertise and a developing pedagogical practice to create a stimulating, research-informed, and supportive learning environment. You will deliver teaching across a range of courses, support student learning and assessment, and contribute to the broader academic and service activities of the School. Key responsibilities include: delivering high-quality teaching using innovative, inclusive, and effective pedagogical practices supporting programme delivery through tutorials, studios, workshops, and assessment activities designing and delivering assessments and providing timely, constructive feedback to students embedding the UC Graduate Profile and encouraging critical, analytical, and creative thinking actively engaging in course evaluation, reflective practice, and continuous improvement contributing to service and administrative activities that support School, Faculty, and University objectives. Mōu | Who You Are You are an enthusiastic educator with expertise in Game Art, Animation or Illustration and a strong commitment to teaching excellence. You are developing your academic career and pedagogical practice, and you enjoy working collaboratively within a creative and collegial environment. You will demonstrate an active commitment to bicultural confidence and competence, including upholding tikanga and values as guided by mana whenua, Ngāi Tūāhuriri, and valuing te reo Māori. Applicants will demonstrate: Evidence of a growing reputation or expertise within a relevant discipline area Experience delivering effective teaching methods for Game Art, Animation, Character Design, that support a diverse range of learners A commitment to reflective practice and ongoing professional development Strong interpersonal skills and the ability to work collaboratively with students and colleagues Competence in digital tools and learning technologies relevant to tertiary teaching Experience with on-line and remote-study course design and deliver technologies An active commitment to UC values, inclusivity, and Te Tiriti o Waitangi. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With The School of Product Design is part of the University of Canterbury’s vibrant academic community and contributes to teaching, research, and innovation that responds to real-world challenges. You will work closely with academic and professional staff within the School and Faculty, contributing to a supportive, collaborative culture that enhances student success and the University’s reputation. For more information, please visit here . Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC Ngā Uara | Our Values of manaakitanga, whanaungatanga and tiakitanga guide our decisions and behaviour and provide a roadmap for how we do things at UC, affirming our commitment to pastoral care and support for our ākonga and staff. They challenge and inspire us to be the best we can, and make UC a great place to work and study. For more info on Ngā Uara | Our Values visit us here . We are committed to accessible higher education, service to the community and the encouragement of talent without barriers of distance, wealth, class, gender or ethnicity. The University explicitly aims to produce graduates and support staff who are engaged with their communities, empowered to act for good and determined to make a difference in the world. For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/ The closing date for this position is: 25 September 2026 (midnight, NZ time) Please note applications will be reviewed after the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted via our careers website and should include a cover letter and resume. Please note, we do not accept applications by email, however we are happy to answer your queries in relation to the application process, please forward these to WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz You must have Aotearoa New Zealand or Australian citizenship/permanent residency to be considered for this role Please note, this is an onsite position. #LI-DNI Job Details Reference # 33408 Posted on 18 Sep 2026 Closes on 25 Sep 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Tertiary Teaching Job level(s) Academic Work type(s) Continuing (Permanent) full-time More details (document) Position description 1 PD_Senior Tutor_Game Arts.pdf Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 9 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance. Loading . . ."
    }
